window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url":"index.html","page":"DSA Field Guide — data structures & algorithms, explained simply","section":"DSA, explained like you're ten.","tool":"home","text":"DSA, explained like you're ten. A visual, beginner-first guide to the coding interview. We take the 45+ problems that actually show up in real interviews, sort them into a handful of patterns , and explain each one with a everyday picture first — then the real algorithm, the Big-O, and Python you ca","keywords":["dsa","explained","like","you","re","ten"]},{"url":"foundations/index.html","page":"How to use this guide","section":"How to use this guide","tool":"foundations","text":"How to use this guide Data structures and algorithms have a reputation for being scary. They don't have to be. This guide takes the small set of ideas that actually show up in interviews and explains each one with a picture first — a everyday story you already understand — before any code or jargon.","keywords":["how","to","use","this","guide"]},{"url":"foundations/index.html#intro","page":"How to use this guide","section":"Who this is for","tool":"foundations","text":"01 Who this is for You've shipped real software for a few years. You can build features, fix bugs, read a stack trace at 2am. But \"invert a binary tree on a whiteboard\" makes your stomach drop — and the usual advice (\"just grind 500 problems\") feels like a wall you'll never climb. This guide is for ","keywords":["who","this","is","for"]},{"url":"foundations/index.html#loop","page":"How to use this guide","section":"The loop you run in an interview","tool":"foundations","text":"02 The loop you run in an interview Strong candidates aren't faster typists. They run the same small loop in their head for every problem. Almost everything in this guide is one step of this picture — keep it in mind as you read. The loop you run in an interview read restate it inputs, output, limit","keywords":["the","loop","you","run","in","an","interview"]},{"url":"foundations/index.html#anatomy","page":"How to use this guide","section":"How a problem page works","tool":"foundations","text":"03 How a problem page works Every pattern and problem page is built the same way, so once you've read one you know how to read them all. Here are the parts you'll meet, top to bottom: The analogy A blue callout with a real-world story, always before any code. Read this first — it carries the whole i","keywords":["how","problem","page","works"]},{"url":"foundations/index.html#order","page":"How to use this guide","section":"A suggested order","tool":"foundations","text":"04 A suggested order Start anywhere you like — but if you want a path that builds gently and never overwhelms you, follow these four steps. 1 Build the foundations Get comfortable with Big-O and the core data structures. This is the vocabulary every later page uses. → Foundations · these 5 short les","keywords":["suggested","order"]},{"url":"foundations/big-o.html","page":"Big-O & complexity","section":"Big-O & complexity","tool":"foundations","text":"Big-O & complexity Big-O sounds like maths homework, but it answers one plain question: as the input gets bigger, how fast does the work pile up? That's it. By the end of this page you'll read it on sight, picture each class with an everyday story, and say the cost of your own code out loud — the th","keywords":["big","complexity"]},{"url":"foundations/big-o.html#what","page":"Big-O & complexity","section":"What Big-O means","tool":"foundations","text":"01 What Big-O means Big-O describes how the amount of work grows as the input grows. It deliberately ignores the small stuff: we drop constant factors and keep only the biggest term, because for a large input that term dwarfs everything else. A recipe for 2 people vs 200 analogy You can cook dinner ","keywords":["what","big","means"]},{"url":"foundations/big-o.html#classes","page":"Big-O & complexity","section":"The complexity zoo","tool":"foundations","text":"02 The complexity zoo You'll meet the same handful of growth curves over and over. Here's each one in a single plain sentence, with a picture to make it stick: O(1) Constant — the work doesn't grow at all. Like flipping a light switch: same effort whether the room is tiny or huge. O(log n) Logarithm","keywords":["the","complexity","zoo"]},{"url":"foundations/big-o.html#space","page":"Big-O & complexity","section":"Time vs space","tool":"foundations","text":"03 Time vs space Big-O measures two different things, and interviewers ask about both. Time complexity is how the number of steps grows. Space complexity is how much extra memory you use as the input grows — beyond the input itself. The classic split is in-place versus making a copy . Reversing a li","keywords":["time","vs","space"]},{"url":"foundations/big-o.html#say-it","page":"Big-O & complexity","section":"How to say it out loud","tool":"foundations","text":"04 How to say it out loud Interview skill Easy ~8 min O(n) vs O(1) lookup Knowing the cost isn't enough — you have to state it. The formula is simple: name the time, give the one-line reason, then name the space and its reason. Reading a contents page vs a phone's contacts analogy To check if a frie","keywords":["how","to","say","it","out","loud","in","a_list","a_set","set","nums","the_set"]},{"url":"foundations/big-o.html#speed-quiz","page":"Big-O & complexity","section":"The Big-O speed quiz","tool":"foundations","text":"05 The Big-O speed quiz In a real interview you get about ten seconds to cost a snippet you've never seen. Train that reflex here: read the code, pick its Big-O before the bar runs out. The bank includes the two classic traps — a nested loop that's secretly linear, and a recursion that's secretly ch","keywords":["the","big","speed","quiz"]},{"url":"foundations/big-o.html#cheat","page":"Big-O & complexity","section":"Want the full cost tables?","tool":"foundations","text":"06 Want the full cost tables? This page gives you the intuition. When you want the exact per-structure costs — array vs hash map vs heap vs tree, for every common operation — they live on one page you can keep open the night before. Keep it nearby The Cheat-sheet & glossary has the full Big-O cost t","keywords":["want","the","full","cost","tables"]},{"url":"foundations/arrays-strings.html","page":"Arrays, strings & hashing","section":"Arrays, strings & hashing","tool":"foundations","text":"Arrays, strings & hashing Before patterns, you need the boxes the patterns play with. An array is a row of equal boxes you can jump into instantly. A string is a row of letters that, in Python, never changes. And hashing — dicts and sets — is a magic coat-check that hands you any item the moment you","keywords":["arrays","strings","hashing"]},{"url":"foundations/arrays-strings.html#arrays","page":"Arrays, strings & hashing","section":"Arrays","tool":"foundations","text":"01 Arrays An array (Python calls it a list ) is a row of equal-sized boxes sitting side by side in one block of memory. Each box has a number — its index — starting at 0. A row of numbered lockers analogy Picture a hallway of identical lockers, numbered 0, 1, 2, 3… To open locker 3 you walk straight","keywords":["arrays","list","nums","append"]},{"url":"foundations/arrays-strings.html#strings","page":"Arrays, strings & hashing","section":"Strings","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Strings A string is a row of characters — like an array of letters. The twist in Python: a string is immutable , meaning it can never be changed once made. Every \"edit\" secretly builds a brand-new string. A word carved into stone analogy A Python string is like a word carved into a stone tablet —","keywords":["strings","word","result","piece","list","append","join","parts","TypeError","chars"]},{"url":"foundations/arrays-strings.html#hashing","page":"Arrays, strings & hashing","section":"Hashing — dicts & sets","tool":"foundations","text":"03 Hashing — dicts & sets A hash table is the secret weapon behind Python's dict (key → value) and set (just keys). It lets you store and look things up in roughly constant time — O(1) on average — no matter how much it holds. The coat-check / sticky-note analogy At a coat-check you hand over your c","keywords":["hashing","dicts","sets","dict","set","key","value","get","default","in","myset","Counter","iterable","a_list","list","insert","deque","bob","absent","nums"]},{"url":"foundations/linked-lists.html","page":"Linked lists, stacks & queues","section":"Linked lists, stacks & queues","tool":"foundations","text":"Linked lists, stacks & queues Arrays love their tidy row, but sometimes you want to add and remove things constantly without shuffling everyone. Enter three flexible helpers: the linked list — a chain you follow link by link; the stack — a pile where the last thing in is the first out; and the queue","keywords":["linked","lists","stacks","queues"]},{"url":"foundations/linked-lists.html#linked","page":"Linked lists, stacks & queues","section":"Linked lists","tool":"foundations","text":"01 Linked lists A linked list is a chain of little boxes called nodes . Each node holds a value plus an arrow — next — pointing to the box after it. The chain ends when an arrow points at nothing ( None ). A treasure hunt of clues analogy Each node is a clue that tells you where the next clue is hid","keywords":["linked","lists","next","None"]},{"url":"foundations/linked-lists.html#stack","page":"Linked lists, stacks & queues","section":"Stacks","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Stacks A stack is a pile where you only ever touch the top. The last thing you put in is the first thing you take out — computer scientists call this LIFO (last-in, first-out). A stack of plates analogy Think of clean plates piled on a counter. You add a plate on top, and you take a plate off the","keywords":["stacks","list","append","pop","stack"]},{"url":"foundations/linked-lists.html#queue","page":"Linked lists, stacks & queues","section":"Queues","tool":"foundations","text":"03 Queues A queue is the opposite manners: the first thing in is the first thing out — FIFO (first-in, first-out). You add at the back and remove from the front, exactly like a fair line. A line at the shop analogy People join a checkout line at the back and are served from the front, in the order t","keywords":["queues","collections","deque","list","pop","append","popleft","from","import","head","node","next","None","while"]},{"url":"foundations/trees-graphs.html","page":"Trees, heaps & graphs","section":"Trees, heaps & graphs","tool":"foundations","text":"Trees, heaps & graphs Lists are flat, but a lot of the world branches. A tree is a family tree — one root, branching into children. A heap is a special tree that always keeps its smallest (or largest) item on top. And a graph is the most general shape of all: dots joined by lines, like a map of frie","keywords":["trees","heaps","graphs"]},{"url":"foundations/trees-graphs.html#trees","page":"Trees, heaps & graphs","section":"Trees","tool":"foundations","text":"01 Trees A tree grows from a single top node called the root . Each node points to children below it; a node with no children is a leaf . The height is how many levels deep it goes. A binary tree is the common kind where every node has at most two children. A family tree / org chart analogy Picture ","keywords":["trees"]},{"url":"foundations/trees-graphs.html#heaps","page":"Trees, heaps & graphs","section":"Heaps","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Heaps A min-heap is a special tree with one simple rule: every parent is smaller than or equal to its children. Follow that rule everywhere and the very smallest item is guaranteed to sit at the top — ready to grab instantly. A tournament where the smallest rises analogy Imagine a knockout tourna","keywords":["heaps","heapq","list","heapify","heappush","heappop"]},{"url":"foundations/trees-graphs.html#graphs","page":"Trees, heaps & graphs","section":"Graphs","tool":"foundations","text":"03 Graphs A graph is just dots (called nodes or vertices ) joined by lines (called edges ). Edges can be two-way, or one-way ( directed ) like a one-way street. It's the most general shape — a tree is simply a graph with no loops. A map of friendships analogy Think of a friendship map: each person i","keywords":["graphs","visited","deque","heapq","heapify","graph","node"]},{"url":"patterns/index.html","page":"The pattern map","section":"The pattern map","tool":"patterns","text":"The pattern map Strong candidates don't memorise hundreds of problems. They learn to recognise which of 16 patterns a question is secretly asking for. This page is the map: read the tells in a problem, then jump straight to the right tool. No code here — just how to choose.","keywords":["the","pattern","map"]},{"url":"patterns/index.html#spot","page":"The pattern map","section":"How to spot a pattern","tool":"patterns","text":"00 How to spot a pattern A coding problem rarely says \"use two pointers\". Instead it leaves clues — the shape of the input, the words it uses, the thing it asks for. The skill is matching those tells (the little symptoms) to the right tool (the pattern). Once you can do that, the code almost writes ","keywords":["how","to","spot","pattern"]},{"url":"patterns/index.html#map","page":"The pattern map","section":"The map","tool":"patterns","text":"01 The map Find the row whose left cell matches the tell you spotted, then click the tool in the middle to open that pattern. The right cell says why that tool fits — the one-line reason worth remembering. If you see… Reach for… Why A sorted array · a pair/triple summing to a target · a palindrome ·","keywords":["the","map"]},{"url":"patterns/index.html#beyond","page":"The pattern map","section":"The map just grew","tool":"patterns","text":"02 The map just grew Earlier editions of this guide left four specialised patterns \"for later\". Later arrived: all four now have full lessons, animations and runnable problems. They round out the senior-interview toolbox — reach for them when their tells show up: Greedy Reach for it when one sorted ","keywords":["the","map","just","grew"]},{"url":"patterns/arrays-hashing.html","page":"Arrays & Hashing","section":"Arrays & Hashing","tool":"patterns","text":"Arrays & Hashing The most common tool in the whole guide. A hash map is instant memory of what you've already seen — ask \"have I met this before?\" and it answers immediately. That one superpower turns many slow \"compare everything to everything\" scans into a single fast pass. Let's build the intuiti","keywords":["arrays","hashing"]},{"url":"patterns/arrays-hashing.html#pattern","page":"Arrays & Hashing","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea A hash map (Python calls it a dict ) is a box of labelled drawers. You file a value under a key , and later you can fetch it back — or just check \"is anything filed under this key?\" — in O(1) , meaning the time doesn't grow as the map gets bigger. Compare that to a plain list, where \"is ","keywords":["the","idea","dict","set"]},{"url":"patterns/arrays-hashing.html#two-sum","page":"Arrays & Hashing","section":"Two Sum","tool":"patterns","text":"01 Two Sum Arrays & Hashing Easy ~10 min O(n) time · O(n) space Given a list of numbers and a target , return the indices of the two numbers that add up to it. For nums = [2, 7, 11, 15] and target = 9 , the answer is [0, 1] because 2 + 7 = 9 . Sticky notes of \"what I still need\" analogy As you meet ","keywords":["two","sum","target","nums","me","seen","need","enumerate","if","in"]},{"url":"patterns/arrays-hashing.html#group-anagrams","page":"Arrays & Hashing","section":"Group Anagrams","tool":"patterns","text":"02 Group Anagrams Arrays & Hashing Medium ~15 min O(n·k log k) time · O(n·k) space Given a list of words, group together the ones that are rearrangements of each other (anagrams). For [\"eat\",\"tea\",\"tan\",\"ate\",\"nat\",\"bat\"] , one grouping is [[\"eat\",\"tea\",\"ate\"], [\"tan\",\"nat\"], [\"bat\"]] . File each wo","keywords":["group","anagrams","eat","tea","tan","ate","nat","bat","aet","defaultdict","list","join","sorted","word","groups","key","log","big","collections","append","values"]},{"url":"patterns/arrays-hashing.html#product-except-self","page":"Arrays & Hashing","section":"Product of Array Except Self","tool":"patterns","text":"03 Product of Array Except Self Arrays & Hashing Medium ~20 min O(n) time · O(1) extra space Return a new array where out[i] is the product of all the numbers except nums[i] — and do it without division . For [1, 2, 3, 4] the answer is [24, 12, 8, 6] . Everything on my left × everything on my right ","keywords":["product","of","array","except","self","out","nums","prefix","suffix","res"]},{"url":"patterns/two-pointers.html","page":"Two Pointers","section":"Two Pointers","tool":"patterns","text":"Two Pointers Some problems become easy the moment you stop using one finger to scan a list and start using two — usually one at each end, walking toward the middle. This single trick turns a slow \"check every pair\" into a fast single pass. Let's see it move, then solve three classic problems with it","keywords":["two","pointers"]},{"url":"patterns/two-pointers.html#pattern","page":"Two Pointers","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea Picture a row of cards on a table. The slow way to compare or rearrange them is to scan with one finger, again and again. The two-pointer way: put one finger ( left ) on the first card and another ( right ) on the last, then move them toward each other. Each step does useful work and the","keywords":["the","idea","left","right"]},{"url":"patterns/two-pointers.html#valid-palindrome","page":"Two Pointers","section":"Valid Palindrome","tool":"patterns","text":"01 Valid Palindrome Two Pointers Easy ~10 min O(n) time · O(1) space A palindrome reads the same forwards and backwards. Given a string, return True if it's a palindrome when you ignore punctuation, spaces, and capital-vs-small letters. Example: \"A man, a plan, a canal: Panama\" → True . Two readers ","keywords":["valid","palindrome","True","man","plan","canal","Panama","left","right","False","isalnum","lower","while"]},{"url":"patterns/two-pointers.html#three-sum","page":"Two Pointers","section":"3Sum","tool":"patterns","text":"02 3Sum Two Pointers + Sort Medium ~20 min O(n²) time · O(1) space Given a list of numbers, find every unique triple that adds up to 0 . For [-1, 0, 1, 2, -1, -4] the answer is [[-1, -1, 2], [-1, 0, 1]] . Lay the cards out in order first analogy Sort the numbers into a neat row, smallest to largest.","keywords":["sum","nums","left","right","end","len","if","and","continue"]},{"url":"patterns/two-pointers.html#container","page":"Two Pointers","section":"Container With Most Water","tool":"patterns","text":"03 Container With Most Water Two Pointers Medium ~15 min O(n) time · O(1) space Each number is the height of a vertical wall. Pick two walls so the water trapped between them is as much as possible. Water held = min(left, right) × (distance between them) . For [1,8,6,2,5,4,8,3,7] the most is 49 . Wa","keywords":["container","with","most","water","min","left","right","distance","between","them","len","best","max"]},{"url":"patterns/sliding-window.html","page":"Sliding Window","section":"Sliding Window","tool":"patterns","text":"Sliding Window When a problem asks for the best, longest, or shortest contiguous run inside a list or string, you don't have to re-check every possible window from scratch. Instead you slide a window along the data — add the new item on the right, drop the old one on the left — and reuse everything ","keywords":["sliding","window"]},{"url":"patterns/sliding-window.html#pattern","page":"Sliding Window","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea A sliding window is just two markers — a left edge and a right edge — that frame a stretch of the data. You grow the window by moving right forward, and you shrink it by moving left forward. The trick is that when the window moves, you only update for the one item that entered and the on","keywords":["the","idea","left","right"]},{"url":"patterns/sliding-window.html#best-time","page":"Sliding Window","section":"Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock","tool":"patterns","text":"01 Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock One-pass greedy Interview note Easy ~10 min O(n) time · O(1) space Say it out loud Interviewers usually call this one-pass greedy (track min buy so far + best profit), not classic sliding window. We keep it on this page because the running window of “days so far” i","keywords":["best","time","to","buy","and","sell","stock","min_price","min","max"]},{"url":"patterns/sliding-window.html#longest-substring","page":"Sliding Window","section":"Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters","tool":"patterns","text":"02 Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters Sliding Window Medium ~20 min O(n) time · O(min(n, alphabet)) space Given a string, find the length of the longest run of characters with no repeats . For \"abcabcbb\" the answer is 3 (the stretch \"abc\" ). For \"bbbbb\" it's 1 . A window of all-different","keywords":["longest","substring","without","repeating","characters","abcabcbb","abc","bbbbb","left","right","seen","ch","best","max"]},{"url":"patterns/sliding-window.html#min-window","page":"Sliding Window","section":"Minimum Window Substring","tool":"patterns","text":"03 Minimum Window Substring Sliding Window Hard ~30 min O(n) time · O(alphabet) space Say it out loud This is the true sliding-window classic : grow right until the window is valid, then shrink left while it stays valid, remembering the shortest. In a loop, say “need / have counts + formed counter,”","keywords":["minimum","window","substring","ADOBECODEBANC","ABC","BANC","need","have","formed","right","left","ch","Counter","best","inf","missing"]},{"url":"patterns/stack.html","page":"Stack","section":"Stack","tool":"patterns","text":"Stack A stack is a pile where you only ever add to the top and remove from the top — last in, first out . That one rule makes it perfect for two jobs: matching things that nest (brackets, tags) and answering \"what's the next bigger thing?\" with a clever variant called the monotonic stack — a tool wo","keywords":["stack"]},{"url":"patterns/stack.html#pattern","page":"Stack","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea A stack has just two moves: push (drop an item on top) and pop (take the top item off). You can only touch the top — never the middle or bottom. Because the most recent thing you added is the first thing you take back, a stack naturally remembers \"what's still open\" or \"what's still wait","keywords":["the","idea","stack","append","pop"]},{"url":"patterns/stack.html#valid-parentheses","page":"Stack","section":"Valid Parentheses","tool":"patterns","text":"01 Valid Parentheses Stack Easy ~10 min O(n) time · O(n) space Given a string of only the brackets () , [] and {} , decide whether every bracket is correctly opened and closed in the right order. \"()[]{}\" is valid; \"(]\" is not; \"([])\" is valid. A pile of opened brackets analogy Every time you open a","keywords":["valid","parentheses","stack","False","return","not"]},{"url":"patterns/stack.html#daily-temperatures","page":"Stack","section":"Daily Temperatures","tool":"patterns","text":"02 Daily Temperatures Monotonic Stack Medium ~20 min O(n) time · O(n) space Given a list of daily temperatures, for each day report how many days you must wait until a warmer day — or 0 if no warmer day ever comes. For [73, 74, 75, 71, 69, 72, 76, 73] the answer is [1, 1, 4, 2, 1, 1, 0, 0] . A queue","keywords":["daily","temperatures","stack","res","temps","while","for"]},{"url":"patterns/stack.html#monotonic","page":"Stack","section":"Name the tool: the monotonic stack","tool":"patterns","text":"03 Name the tool: the monotonic stack Daily Temperatures wasn't a one-off trick — it's an instance of a named, reusable template that interviewers expect seniors to know by name : a stack you keep sorted by popping anyone who'd break the order . Saying \"this is a monotonic stack problem\" out loud in","keywords":["name","the","tool","monotonic","stack","indices","kept","sorted","by","value","for","in","enumerate","items","while","and","knob","comparison","pop","ANSWER","FOR","IS","or","record","it","here","append"]},{"url":"patterns/stack.html#largest-rectangle","page":"Stack","section":"Largest Rectangle in Histogram","tool":"patterns","text":"04 Largest Rectangle in Histogram Stack Hard ~30 min O(n) time · O(n) space Given bar heights (every bar has width 1), find the area of the largest rectangle that fits entirely inside the histogram. For [2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 3] the answer is 10 . Let's be honest up front: this is a famous Hard — but it's ","keywords":["largest","rectangle","in","histogram","top","heights","stack","height","width"]},{"url":"patterns/binary-search.html","page":"Binary Search","section":"Binary Search","tool":"patterns","text":"Binary Search When a list is already sorted , you never have to check every item. Look at the middle, decide which half the answer lives in, and throw the other half away. Each peek halves what's left — so even a million items take only about twenty looks. Let's watch it work, then solve three class","keywords":["binary","search"]},{"url":"patterns/binary-search.html#pattern","page":"Binary Search","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea Binary search works on a sorted list. You keep two markers — lo (the lowest spot the answer could be) and hi (the highest). Look at the middle item between them. If it's your target, you're done. If the middle is too small, the answer must be to the right, so drop the whole left half; if","keywords":["the","idea","lo","hi"]},{"url":"patterns/binary-search.html#binary-search","page":"Binary Search","section":"Binary Search","tool":"patterns","text":"01 Binary Search Binary Search Easy ~10 min O(log n) time · O(1) space Given a sorted list of numbers and a target , return the index where the target sits, or -1 if it isn't there. For [-1, 0, 3, 5, 9, 12] and target 9 , the answer is 4 . A \"higher or lower?\" guessing game analogy A friend picks a ","keywords":["binary","search","target","lo","hi","mid","nums","log","while"]},{"url":"patterns/binary-search.html#search-rotated","page":"Binary Search","section":"Search in Rotated Sorted Array","tool":"patterns","text":"02 Search in Rotated Sorted Array Binary Search Medium ~25 min O(log n) time · O(1) space A sorted list was rotated at some unknown spot — for example [0,1,2,4,5,6,7] becomes [4,5,6,7,0,1,2] . Given the rotated list and a target , return its index, or -1 . Still aim for O(log n) . A deck of cards, c","keywords":["search","in","rotated","sorted","array","target","log","mid","nums","lo","hi"]},{"url":"patterns/binary-search.html#bsearch-answer","page":"Binary Search","section":"Binary search on the answer","tool":"patterns","text":"03 Binary search on the answer Here's the senior move: the haystack doesn't have to be an array at all. If you can ask a yes/no question whose answers form NNN…YYY as some number grows — \"can speed k finish in time?\" (if yes for k , it's yes for every faster k too) — then you can binary-search the n","keywords":["binary","search","on","the","answer","NNN","YYY","lo","hi","smallest","possible","largest","mid","works","return"]},{"url":"patterns/binary-search.html#koko","page":"Binary Search","section":"Koko Eating Bananas","tool":"patterns","text":"04 Koko Eating Bananas Binary Search Medium ~20 min O(n log m) time · O(1) space Koko has piles of bananas and h hours. Each hour she picks one pile and eats up to k bananas from it — if the pile runs out early, the rest of that hour is wasted. Find the minimum integer speed k that finishes everythi","keywords":["koko","eating","bananas","hours","ceil","lo","hi","max","piles","mid","NNN","YYY","works"]},{"url":"patterns/linked-list.html","page":"Linked List","section":"Linked List","tool":"patterns","text":"Linked List A linked list is a chain of little boxes, each holding a value and an arrow ( next ) pointing to the box behind it. You can't jump to \"item 5\" — you can only follow arrows. So these problems are pointer surgery : carefully re-hook the arrows, often with a spare dummy box at the front, or","keywords":["linked","list"]},{"url":"patterns/linked-list.html#pattern","page":"Linked List","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea Each box is a node : it stores a value and a single arrow, next , to the following node. The last node's arrow points at None (nothing). Because there's no index, almost every trick comes down to one of three moves: relink arrows one at a time (always saving the next box before you overw","keywords":["the","idea","next","None","cur","prev","nxt","if","dummy"]},{"url":"patterns/linked-list.html#reverse","page":"Linked List","section":"Reverse Linked List","tool":"patterns","text":"01 Reverse Linked List Linked List Easy ~10 min O(n) time · O(1) space Given the head of a linked list like 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5 , flip all the arrows so it becomes 5 → 4 → 3 → 2 → 1 , and return the new head. Do it in place. Walk the chain, flipping each arrow analogy Walk along the chain and turn eac","keywords":["reverse","linked","list","prev","cur","next","None","nxt","head","build","to_list"]},{"url":"patterns/linked-list.html#cycle","page":"Linked List","section":"Linked List Cycle","tool":"patterns","text":"02 Linked List Cycle Fast & Slow Pointers Easy ~15 min O(n) time · O(1) space Given the head of a linked list, decide whether it has a cycle — does some node's arrow point back into the chain, forming a loop, instead of eventually reaching None ? Return True or False , using only O(1) extra space. T","keywords":["linked","list","cycle","None","True","False","slow","fast","next","is","and","while","head"]},{"url":"patterns/linked-list.html#merge","page":"Linked List","section":"Merge Two Sorted Lists","tool":"patterns","text":"03 Merge Two Sorted Lists Linked List + Dummy Head Easy ~15 min O(n+m) time · O(1) space Given two sorted linked lists, weave them into one sorted list and return its head. For 1 → 2 → 4 and 1 → 3 → 4 , the result is 1 → 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 4 . Merging two sorted piles of cards analogy You have two face","keywords":["merge","two","sorted","lists","dummy","tail","next","l1","or","l2","if","ListNode","while","and"]},{"url":"patterns/trees-traversal.html","page":"Trees · BFS & DFS","section":"Trees · BFS & DFS","tool":"patterns","text":"Trees · BFS & DFS A surprising number of tree problems are really just a walk : visit every node in some order and do a little work at each one. There are two famous ways to walk — go deep first (recursion), or go wide first (a queue). Learn these two strolls and a whole shelf of tree questions fall","keywords":["trees","bfs","dfs"]},{"url":"patterns/trees-traversal.html#pattern","page":"Trees · BFS & DFS","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea A tree is just nodes connected in branches: a top node (the root ), and each node points to a few children below it. To answer almost anything — \"how tall is it?\", \"is it sorted?\", \"what's between these two nodes?\" — you visit the nodes in a careful order. There are two orders worth know","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"patterns/trees-traversal.html#max-depth","page":"Trees · BFS & DFS","section":"Maximum Depth of Binary Tree","tool":"patterns","text":"01 Maximum Depth of Binary Tree Trees · DFS Easy ~10 min O(n) time · O(h) space The depth (or height) of a tree is the number of nodes on the longest path from the root down to a leaf. For the tree [3, 9, 20, null, null, 15, 7] the answer is 3 (the path 3 → 20 → 15 ). Ask your kids how deep they are","keywords":["maximum","depth","of","binary","tree","null","the","taller","its","two","children","depths","max_depth","node","None","max","left_depth","right_depth","if","not","root","return","left","right"]},{"url":"patterns/trees-traversal.html#level-order","page":"Trees · BFS & DFS","section":"Binary Tree Level-Order Traversal (BFS)","tool":"patterns","text":"02 Binary Tree Level-Order Traversal (BFS) Trees · BFS Medium ~20 min O(n) time · O(n) space Return the node values grouped level by level , top to bottom. For [3, 9, 20, null, null, 15, 7] the answer is [[3], [9, 20], [15, 7]] . A queue at a ticket counter analogy Picture a queue — a line where you","keywords":["binary","tree","level","order","traversal","bfs","null","deque","while","queue","len","from","collections","import","for","in","range"]},{"url":"patterns/trees-traversal.html#validate-bst","page":"Trees · BFS & DFS","section":"Validate Binary Search Tree","tool":"patterns","text":"03 Validate Binary Search Tree Trees · DFS Medium ~20 min O(n) time · O(h) space A binary search tree (BST) keeps everything sorted: for every node, all values on its left are smaller and all on its right are larger. Given a tree, return True if it obeys that rule everywhere. A shrinking room of all","keywords":["validate","binary","search","tree","True","low","high","False","node","val","infinity","left","right","null","ok","float","inf"]},{"url":"patterns/trees-traversal.html#lca","page":"Trees · BFS & DFS","section":"Lowest Common Ancestor of a BST","tool":"patterns","text":"04 Lowest Common Ancestor of a BST Trees · BST Medium ~15 min O(h) time · O(1) space The lowest common ancestor (LCA) of two nodes is the deepest node that has both of them somewhere below it (a node can be its own ancestor). In a BST , the sorted order makes this fast. The fork in the road analogy ","keywords":["lowest","common","ancestor","of","bst","cur","val","return"]},{"url":"patterns/heap-topk.html","page":"Heap / Top-K","section":"Heap / Top-K","tool":"patterns","text":"Heap / Top-K When a problem asks for the K biggest or smallest things, or keeps asking you to grab the current min or max, sorting the whole list is overkill. A heap gives you the smallest (or largest) item instantly and stays cheap to update. Let's see what a heap looks like, then use it on two cla","keywords":["heap","top"]},{"url":"patterns/heap-topk.html#pattern","page":"Heap / Top-K","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea A heap is a special tree kept in a simple rule: every parent is no bigger than its children (a min-heap ) — or no smaller, for a max-heap . That one rule means the smallest item is always sitting right at the top, free to read. Adding or removing an item only nudges one value up or down ","keywords":["the","idea","sort","heapq","heappush","heappop","heap"]},{"url":"patterns/heap-topk.html#kth-largest","page":"Heap / Top-K","section":"Kth Largest Element in an Array","tool":"patterns","text":"01 Kth Largest Element in an Array Heap / Top-K Medium ~15 min O(n log k) time · O(k) space Given a list of numbers and a number k , return the k-th largest value (by rank, not the k-th distinct value). For [3, 2, 1, 5, 6, 4] with k = 2 , the 2nd largest is 5 . A shelf that holds only the K biggest ","keywords":["kth","largest","element","in","an","array","heapq","heappush","heappop","heap","import","if","len"]},{"url":"patterns/heap-topk.html#top-k-frequent","page":"Heap / Top-K","section":"Top K Frequent Elements","tool":"patterns","text":"02 Top K Frequent Elements Heap / Top-K Medium ~20 min O(n log n) time · O(n) space Given a list of numbers, return the k values that appear most often. For [1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3] with k = 2 , the two most frequent are [1, 2] . Tally the votes, then pick the winners analogy First hold an election: walk ","keywords":["top","frequent","elements","collections","Counter","most_common","value","count","from","import","counts","nums"]},{"url":"patterns/backtracking.html","page":"Backtracking","section":"Backtracking","tool":"patterns","text":"Backtracking Some problems ask for every combination, subset, or arrangement — and the only honest way is to try them all. Backtracking does this without getting lost: make one choice, dig deeper, then undo that choice and try the next. You explore a whole tree of decisions and leave no mess behind ","keywords":["backtracking"]},{"url":"patterns/backtracking.html#pattern","page":"Backtracking","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea Imagine you're exploring a maze while unrolling a ball of string behind you. At every fork you pick a path and keep going. If it dead-ends, you don't panic — you rewind the string back to the last fork and try a different path. Backtracking is exactly that, in code: at each step you make","keywords":["the","idea","choose","recurse","un","path"]},{"url":"patterns/backtracking.html#subsets","page":"Backtracking","section":"Subsets","tool":"patterns","text":"01 Subsets Backtracking Medium ~15 min O(n·2ⁿ) time · O(n) space Given a list of distinct numbers, return every possible subset (the \"power set\") — including the empty set and the full set. For [1, 2, 3] there are 8 subsets, from [] up to [1, 2, 3] . Pack a bag, one item at a time analogy As in the ","keywords":["subsets","backtrack","start","path","nums","pop","append"]},{"url":"patterns/backtracking.html#combination-sum","page":"Backtracking","section":"Combination Sum","tool":"patterns","text":"02 Combination Sum Backtracking Medium ~25 min exponential time · O(target) space Given a list of distinct positive numbers and a target , find every combination that sums to the target. The catch: each number may be reused as many times as you like. For candidates [2, 3, 6, 7] and target 7 , the an","keywords":["combination","sum","target","backtrack","start","path","remain","candidates","pop"]},{"url":"patterns/graphs.html","page":"Graphs","section":"Graphs","tool":"patterns","text":"Graphs A graph is just dots joined by lines: the dots are nodes (things) and the lines are edges (connections). Once you can explore one, a whole shelf of problems opens up — including many \"grid\" puzzles that are secretly graphs in disguise. Let's learn the two ways to walk a graph, then solve thre","keywords":["graphs"]},{"url":"patterns/graphs.html#pattern","page":"Graphs","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea Picture a map of towns joined by roads. Each town is a node ; each road is an edge . Almost every graph question is really one question: \"starting here, how do I visit everywhere I can reach?\" You have two ways to wander. BFS Breadth-first search uses a queue (a waiting line). It explore","keywords":["the","idea","seen"]},{"url":"patterns/graphs.html#num-islands","page":"Graphs","section":"Number of Islands","tool":"patterns","text":"01 Number of Islands Graphs · Flood Fill Medium ~20 min O(rows·cols) time · O(rows·cols) space You're given a grid of \"1\" s (land) and \"0\" s (water). Count the islands — groups of land cells joined up/down/left/right. Diagonals don't connect. Spot land, then flood it analogy Walk the grid like readi","keywords":["number","of","islands","count","seen","visited","sink"]},{"url":"patterns/graphs.html#clone-graph","page":"Graphs","section":"Clone Graph","tool":"patterns","text":"02 Clone Graph Graphs · DFS Medium ~20 min O(V+E) time · O(V) space Given one node of a connected graph, build a deep copy : brand-new nodes with the same values, wired up the same way. The copy must share no nodes with the original. Photocopy a friendship network analogy Imagine copying a circle of","keywords":["clone","graph","clones","dfs","node","if","in","return","neighbors","neighbor","True"]},{"url":"patterns/graphs.html#course-schedule","page":"Graphs","section":"Course Schedule","tool":"patterns","text":"03 Course Schedule Graphs · Topological Sort Medium ~25 min O(V+E) time · O(V+E) space You have num_courses courses and a list of prerequisite pairs [a, b] meaning \"take b before a \". Can you finish them all? In graph terms: is the dependency graph free of cycles ? Only take a course once its prereq","keywords":["course","schedule","num_courses","prereq","indegree","done","deque","True","False"]},{"url":"patterns/graphs.html#dijkstra","page":"Graphs","section":"Network Delay Time","tool":"patterns","text":"04 Network Delay Time Dijkstra Medium ~30 min O((V+E) log V) time · O(V+E) space Say it out loud “Weighted shortest paths from one source → Dijkstra with a min-heap of (dist, node). Answer is the max distance among reachable nodes, or −1 if someone never hears the signal.” You have n network nodes a","keywords":["network","delay","time","times","dist","max","heapq","node","values"]},{"url":"patterns/dynamic-programming.html","page":"Dynamic Programming","section":"Dynamic Programming","tool":"patterns","text":"Dynamic Programming Dynamic programming (DP) sounds scary, but it's one small idea: break a big problem into smaller versions of itself, solve each tiny piece once, and reuse those answers instead of computing them again. It turns slow, repetitive recursion into a quick fill-in-the-blanks. Let's wat","keywords":["dynamic","programming"]},{"url":"patterns/dynamic-programming.html#pattern","page":"Dynamic Programming","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea Suppose answering \"step 5\" needs the answers to \"step 4\" and \"step 3\", and those need smaller steps still. A naive recursion recomputes \"step 2\" over and over. DP says: write each small answer down once, then just look it up. You build from the smallest pieces up to the one you want. You","keywords":["the","idea","dp"]},{"url":"patterns/dynamic-programming.html#families","page":"Dynamic Programming","section":"The DP families","tool":"patterns","text":"01 The DP families Senior interviews don't ask \"do you know DP?\" — the interviewer watches whether you recognize which family a problem belongs to, because the family dictates the state shape : what dp[…] means and how many dimensions it needs. Name the family out loud and the recurrence usually wri","keywords":["the","dp","families","amount","cap","fib"]},{"url":"patterns/dynamic-programming.html#climbing-stairs","page":"Dynamic Programming","section":"Climbing Stairs","tool":"patterns","text":"02 Climbing Stairs DP · 1-D Easy ~10 min O(n) time · O(1) space You're climbing a staircase of n steps. Each move you take either 1 step or 2. How many distinct ways can you reach the top? For n = 5 the answer is 8 . Where did your last step come from? analogy To be standing on step n , your final m","keywords":["climbing","stairs","ways"]},{"url":"patterns/dynamic-programming.html#house-robber","page":"Dynamic Programming","section":"House Robber","tool":"patterns","text":"03 House Robber DP · 1-D Medium ~15 min O(n) time · O(1) space A row of houses each hold some money. You can't rob two adjacent houses (their alarms are linked). What's the most money you can grab? For [2, 7, 9, 3, 1] it's 12 (rob houses 2, 9, and 1). At each house, skip or rob analogy Walk down the","keywords":["house","robber","best","max","money","prev","cur"]},{"url":"patterns/dynamic-programming.html#coin-change","page":"Dynamic Programming","section":"Coin Change","tool":"patterns","text":"04 Coin Change DP · Unbounded Medium ~25 min O(amount·coins) time · O(amount) space Given coin values and a target amount , find the fewest coins that add up to it (you have unlimited coins of each value), or −1 if it can't be made. For coins [1, 2, 5] and amount 11 the answer is 3 (5 + 5 + 1). Buil","keywords":["coin","change","amount","dp","min","INF"]},{"url":"patterns/dynamic-programming.html#lis","page":"Dynamic Programming","section":"Longest Increasing Subsequence","tool":"patterns","text":"05 Longest Increasing Subsequence DP · Subsequence Medium ~25 min O(n²) time · O(n) space Find the length of the longest subsequence (skips allowed, order kept) that strictly increases. For [10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18] the answer is 4 — for example 2, 5, 7, 101 or 2, 3, 7, 18 . Whose chain can I ext","keywords":["longest","increasing","subsequence","101","dp","nums","max","tails","len","for","in","bisect","bisect_left","first","tail","that","is","not","smaller","than","if","append","else","same","chain","length","return","range"]},{"url":"patterns/dynamic-programming.html#partition","page":"Dynamic Programming","section":"Partition Equal Subset Sum","tool":"patterns","text":"06 Partition Equal Subset Sum DP · Knapsack Medium ~25 min O(n·S) time · O(S) space Can the list be split into two halves with equal sums ? For [1, 5, 11, 5] the answer is True — 11 on one side, 1 + 5 + 5 on the other. Reframe it in one line and the problem collapses: \"is there a subset summing to t","keywords":["partition","equal","subset","sum","True","False","target","total","reachable","num","dp","or","for","in","if"]},{"url":"patterns/dynamic-programming.html#unique-paths","page":"Dynamic Programming","section":"Unique Paths","tool":"patterns","text":"07 Unique Paths 2-D DP Medium ~20 min O(m·n) time · O(m·n) space Say it out loud “ dp[r][c] = ways to reach this cell = ways from above + ways from left. First row and first column are all 1s.” Name the 2-D grid family. A robot on an m × n grid starts at the top-left and may only move right or down ","keywords":["unique","paths","dp","for","in","range"]},{"url":"patterns/intervals.html","page":"Intervals","section":"Intervals","tool":"patterns","text":"Intervals Lots of problems are really about ranges that might overlap — meetings on a calendar, time slots, booked rooms. The secret that unlocks almost all of them is the same: sort by start time first . Once the ranges are lined up, a single left-to-right pass is enough to merge them, count clashe","keywords":["intervals"]},{"url":"patterns/intervals.html#pattern","page":"Intervals","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea An interval is just a range with a start and an end, like a meeting from 1 to 3. When you have a pile of them, two ranges overlap if one begins before the other ends. Checking every pair is slow. The trick: sort the intervals by their start time . Now they march left to right in order, s","keywords":["the","idea","start","end"]},{"url":"patterns/intervals.html#merge-intervals","page":"Intervals","section":"Merge Intervals","tool":"patterns","text":"01 Merge Intervals Intervals + Sort Medium ~20 min O(n log n) time · O(n) space Given a list of intervals, merge any that overlap and return the tidy list. For [[1,3], [2,6], [8,10], [15,18]] the first two overlap, so the answer is [[1,6], [8,10], [15,18]] . Gluing overlapping strips of tape analogy","keywords":["merge","intervals","merged","start","end","max","old","new","sort","key","lambda","and","append","if"]},{"url":"patterns/intervals.html#meeting-rooms-ii","page":"Intervals","section":"Meeting Rooms II","tool":"patterns","text":"02 Meeting Rooms II Intervals + Heap Medium ~25 min O(n log n) time · O(n) space Say it out loud “Sort by start, keep a min-heap of end times for rooms in use. When the next meeting starts after the earliest end, reuse that room; else open a new one. Answer = peak heap size.” Given meeting time inte","keywords":["meeting","rooms","ii","start","end","heap","best","max","len","intervals","sort","key","lambda","heappop","heappush"]},{"url":"patterns/greedy.html","page":"Greedy","section":"Greedy","tool":"patterns","text":"Greedy Take the best choice available right now , commit to it, and never look back — no undo, no regrets. When that's safe it gives stunningly simple one-pass solutions; the real skill (and what interviews actually test) is knowing when it's safe. Let's build that instinct, then solve three classic","keywords":["greedy"]},{"url":"patterns/greedy.html#pattern","page":"Greedy","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea Most patterns in this guide are about how to move through data. Greedy is different: it's a decision rule . At every step, pick whatever looks best at this exact moment, lock it in, and move on. No backtracking, no second-guessing, no table of remembered sub-answers. When it works, the c","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"patterns/greedy.html#jump-game","page":"Greedy","section":"Jump Game","tool":"patterns","text":"01 Jump Game Greedy Medium ~15 min O(n) time · O(1) space Each slot in the list holds the maximum distance you may jump forward from it (you can always jump less). Starting at index 0, can you reach the last index? Example: [2,3,1,1,4] → True , but [3,2,1,0,4] → False . Stepping stones in a river an","keywords":["jump","game","True","False","reach","max","nums","last"]},{"url":"patterns/greedy.html#gas-station","page":"Greedy","section":"Gas Station","tool":"patterns","text":"02 Gas Station Greedy Medium ~20 min O(n) time · O(1) space There's a circular route of gas stations: gas[i] is the fuel you gain at station i , and cost[i] is the fuel it takes to drive to the next station. Starting with an empty tank, return the index of the starting station from which you can com","keywords":["gas","station","cost","total","tank","start"]},{"url":"patterns/greedy.html#erase-overlap","page":"Greedy","section":"Non-overlapping Intervals","tool":"patterns","text":"03 Non-overlapping Intervals Greedy Medium ~20 min O(n log n) time · O(1) space Given a list of intervals, remove the minimum number of them so the rest never overlap. Example: [[1,2],[2,3],[3,4],[1,3]] → 1 (remove [1,3] ). Note that touching endpoints — [1,2] and [2,3] — do not count as overlapping","keywords":["non","overlapping","intervals","prev_end","start"]},{"url":"patterns/tries.html","page":"Tries","section":"Tries","tool":"patterns","text":"Tries Type two letters into a search box and autocomplete answers instantly — whether the dictionary behind it holds a hundred words or a hundred million. The trick is a trie (prefix tree): a tree where words physically share their common beginnings, so answering \"what starts with ca ?\" costs only a","keywords":["tries"]},{"url":"patterns/tries.html#pattern","page":"Tries","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea A trie stores words the way a hotel stores guests: not in one big bag, but along hallways of letters . To find a word you walk its letters, one hallway per letter, starting from the lobby (the root). Words that begin the same way walk the very same hallways — they only split where their ","keywords":["the","idea","end","app","apple","insert","word","search","startsWith","prefix","ad","True","False"]},{"url":"patterns/tries.html#implement-trie","page":"Tries","section":"Implement Trie (Prefix Tree)","tool":"patterns","text":"01 Implement Trie (Prefix Tree) Tries Medium ~20 min O(L) per op Build the hotel yourself: a class with insert(word) , search(word) (exact words only — the end flag matters!) and startsWith(prefix) . All input is lowercase letters. Insert cat, car, card — then search and watch the end flags work Tur","keywords":["implement","trie","prefix","tree","insert","word","search","startsWith","TrieNode","children","end","False","setdefault","True","_walk","None","node","ch","starts_with","app"]},{"url":"patterns/tries.html#add-search","page":"Tries","section":"Design Add & Search Words","tool":"patterns","text":"02 Design Add & Search Words Tries Medium ~20 min O(L) add · O(26^d) worst search Build add_word(word) and search(word) — but the search word may contain \".\" , which matches any letter. After adding \"bad\" , \"dad\" , \"mad\" : search(\"pad\") → False , search(\".ad\") → True , search(\"b..\") → True . A skele","keywords":["design","add","search","words","add_word","word","bad","dad","mad","pad","False","ad","True","dfs","node","len","end","any","child","for","in","children","values","ba"]},{"url":"patterns/tries.html#word-search-ii","page":"Tries","section":"Word Search II","tool":"patterns","text":"03 Word Search II Tries Hard ~30 min stretch goal Given a grid of letters and a list of words, find every word you can trace through adjacent cells (up/down/left/right), without reusing a cell within one word. The senior twist: don't search the board once per word — search it once for all words simu","keywords":["word","search","ii","dfs","node","nxt","children","get","board","None"]},{"url":"patterns/union-find.html","page":"Union-Find","section":"Union-Find","tool":"patterns","text":"Union-Find Some problems keep asking one little question over and over: \"are these two in the same group?\" — while the groups themselves keep merging . Friend circles grow, islands get bridged, wires get connected. Union-Find (also called Disjoint Set Union) answers that question in what is basicall","keywords":["union","find"]},{"url":"patterns/union-find.html#pattern","page":"Union-Find","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea Clubs with presidents analogy Every kid in school belongs to exactly one club, and every club has exactly one president . To check whether two kids are in the same club, each kid walks up the chain — \"who do you report to?\" — until they reach someone who reports to themselves . That self","keywords":["the","idea","parent","find","union","small","big"]},{"url":"patterns/union-find.html#provinces","page":"Union-Find","section":"Number of Provinces","tool":"patterns","text":"01 Number of Provinces Union-Find Medium ~20 min O(n² α) time · O(n) space There are n cities and an adjacency matrix isConnected , where isConnected[i][j] = 1 means cities i and j are directly connected. A province is a group of cities connected directly or indirectly. Count the provinces. Example:","keywords":["number","of","provinces","isConnected","parent","list","range","find","union","True","count"]},{"url":"patterns/union-find.html#redundant","page":"Union-Find","section":"Redundant Connection","tool":"patterns","text":"02 Redundant Connection Union-Find Medium ~20 min O(n α) time · O(n) space You're given a graph that started as a tree and then got exactly one extra edge . The edges arrive in order. Return the edge that closes the cycle — the last edge that connects two already-connected nodes. Example: [[1,2],[1,","keywords":["redundant","connection","union","False","find"]},{"url":"patterns/union-find.html#accounts-merge","page":"Union-Find","section":"Accounts Merge","tool":"patterns","text":"03 Accounts Merge Union-Find Hard ~30 min O(E α + E log E) Each account is a list [name, email1, email2, …] . Two accounts belong to the same person if they share any email — transitively! Names repeat across different people, so they prove nothing. Merge the accounts and output each person as [name","keywords":["accounts","merge","name","email1","email2","sorted","unique","emails","email","find","John","parent","setdefault","union","defaultdict","list","j2"]},{"url":"patterns/design.html","page":"Design · LRU Cache","section":"Design","tool":"patterns","text":"Design Some interview prompts don't ask you to compute an answer — they ask you to build a small API with hard speed guarantees. The classic is LRU Cache: get and put must both be O(1). The trick is pairing a hash map (instant lookup) with a doubly linked list (instant reorder and eviction).","keywords":["design"]},{"url":"patterns/design.html#pattern","page":"Design · LRU Cache","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea A design problem gives you methods and constraints (“O(1) get/put”, “support undo”, “median in a stream”) and you invent the supporting data structure. Almost always the answer is two structures working together — one for fast lookup, one for order or ranking. A coat check with a queue l","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"patterns/design.html#lru-cache","page":"Design · LRU Cache","section":"LRU Cache","tool":"patterns","text":"01 LRU Cache HashMap + DLL Medium ~35 min O(1) get/put · O(capacity) space Say it out loud “Hash map from key → node, plus a doubly linked list ordered MRU → LRU. get / put move the node to the head; on overflow, evict the tail.” Design a cache with capacity capacity that supports get(key) and put(k","keywords":["lru","cache","get","put","capacity","key","value","head","tail","map","node","prev","dict","OrderedDict","_remove","_add_to_head"]},{"url":"patterns/bits.html","page":"Bit Manipulation","section":"Bit Manipulation","tool":"patterns","text":"Bit Manipulation Every number is secretly a row of on/off switches , and three tiny tricks on that row solve a surprising family of interview problems in O(1) space. Learn the switch-board view once, and operators that looked like alien runes — ^ , & , << — turn into plain sentences.","keywords":["bit","manipulation"]},{"url":"patterns/bits.html#pattern","page":"Bit Manipulation","section":"The idea","tool":"patterns","text":"00 The idea Inside the computer, the number 13 isn't a \"13\" at all — it's the binary row 1101 . Read it like a switch panel labelled 8, 4, 2, 1: the 8-switch, the 4-switch and the 1-switch are on, and 8 + 4 + 1 = 13. Every bit operator is just a rule for combining or sliding two rows of switches. A ","keywords":["the","idea","b0110","b0011","b0010","b0111","b0101","b1010"]},{"url":"patterns/bits.html#single-number","page":"Bit Manipulation","section":"Single Number","tool":"patterns","text":"01 Single Number Bits Easy ~10 min O(n) time · O(1) space Every number in the list appears exactly twice , except one loner — find it in O(n) time and O(1) space . The space bound is the whole puzzle: a counting dict finds it easily, but that's O(n) extra memory. Glow-sticks at a party analogy Every","keywords":["single","number","functools","reduce","operator","xor","nums","acc"]},{"url":"patterns/bits.html#hamming-weight","page":"Bit Manipulation","section":"Number of 1 Bits","tool":"patterns","text":"02 Number of 1 Bits Bits Easy ~10 min O(k) time (k = on-bits) Count the on-switches in a number — its Hamming weight . 13 is 1101 , so the answer is 3. The clever loop runs once per ON bit, not once per bit. Slam off the lowest switch analogy Instead of walking the wall and checking all 32 switches ","keywords":["number","of","bits","bin","count"]},{"url":"patterns/bits.html#counting-bits","page":"Bit Manipulation","section":"Counting Bits","tool":"patterns","text":"03 Counting Bits Bits Medium ~20 min O(n) time For every i from 0 to n, output how many 1-bits i has — in O(n) total , not O(n log n). The trick is realizing the answers reuse each other : this is bits meeting Dynamic Programming . Every row is a shorter row plus maybe one switch analogy i 's switch","keywords":["counting","bits","dp"]},{"url":"drill/index.html","page":"Practice — Timed coding drills","section":"Timed coding drills.","tool":"drill","text":"Timed coding drills. Reading a pattern and solving under a clock are different skills. Each drill here is a 40-minute coding round: five phases, interviewer-style nudges, a senior grading rubric, and a model answer at the end. Your scores stay on this device.","keywords":["timed","coding","drills"]},{"url":"drill/index.html#how","page":"Practice — Timed coding drills","section":"How a 40-minute round runs","tool":"drill","text":"01 How a 40-minute round runs Real coding rounds reward pacing as much as cleverness. Candidates who jump straight to code lose points they never knew existed. The drill walks you through the same five phases an interviewer expects, with a per-phase budget that auto-advances when time is up: Phase B","keywords":["how","minute","round","runs"]},{"url":"drill/index.html#drills","page":"Practice — Timed coding drills","section":"Pick a question","tool":"drill","text":"02 Pick a question","keywords":["pick","question"]},{"url":"drill/index.html#grading","page":"Practice — Timed coding drills","section":"How to self-grade honestly","tool":"drill","text":"03 How to self-grade honestly The rubric only works if you grade what you actually produced , not what you know you could have said. Three rules keep the signal honest: Out loud or on paper counts; in your head doesn't. If you thought \"this is a monotonic stack\" but never said or wrote it, the line ","keywords":["how","to","self","grade","honestly","if","target","in","seen"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html","page":"Cheat-sheet & glossary","section":"Cheat-sheet & glossary","tool":"reference","text":"Cheat-sheet & glossary This is the page you skim the night before an interview. No new lessons — just the numbers and names you want at your fingertips: a quick self-test, how fast each Big-O grows, what every data-structure operation costs (tries and union-find included), the five bit tricks, a one","keywords":["cheat","sheet","glossary"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#self-test","page":"Cheat-sheet & glossary","section":"Self-test: name the pattern","tool":"reference","text":"01 Self-test: name the pattern Before you peek at the tables — can you still name the pattern cold?","keywords":["self","test","name","the","pattern"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#growth","page":"Cheat-sheet & glossary","section":"How fast things grow","tool":"reference","text":"02 How fast things grow Big-O is a speed label. It tells you how the work grows as the input gets bigger — not the exact seconds, just the shape of the growth. The table below shows roughly how many steps each shape takes for a list of 10, 100, and 1000 items. Watch how the bottom rows explode while","keywords":["how","fast","things","grow"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#structures","page":"Cheat-sheet & glossary","section":"Cost per operation, by data structure","tool":"reference","text":"03 Cost per operation, by data structure Each container is good at some things and slow at others — like a toolbox where the hammer is great for nails and useless for screws. Pick the structure whose cheap operation matches what your problem does most. \"Amortised\" means it's occasionally slow but ch","keywords":["cost","per","operation","by","data","structure","join","list","collections","deque"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#bit-tricks","page":"Cheat-sheet & glossary","section":"Bit tricks at a glance","tool":"reference","text":"04 Bit tricks at a glance The whole bit-manipulation pattern runs on a handful of one-liners. Memorise these five and most bit problems open up. Trick What it does x ^ x == 0 pairs cancel — XOR a value with itself and it vanishes x ^ 0 == x identity — XOR with zero changes nothing n & (n - 1) clears","keywords":["bit","tricks","at","glance"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#patterns","page":"Cheat-sheet & glossary","section":"Which pattern when (quick reference)","tool":"reference","text":"05 Which pattern when (quick reference) Half the battle is recognising which tool the question is begging for. Each row below is a quick tell — a few words in the prompt — and the pattern it usually points to. Skim the tell, jump to the page. The tell (what the prompt says) Reach for \"count / group ","keywords":["which","pattern","when","quick","reference"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#pitfalls","page":"Cheat-sheet & glossary","section":"Interview pitfalls","tool":"reference","text":"06 Interview pitfalls Most wrong answers don't come from a missing idea — they come from a small slip. These are the ones that catch nearly everyone, so check them before you say \"done\". Off-by-one in binary search Use a loop while lo <= hi , and always move past the middle: lo = mid + 1 or hi = mid","keywords":["interview","pitfalls","lo","hi","mid","for","in","items","while"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#glossary","page":"Cheat-sheet & glossary","section":"Glossary","tool":"reference","text":"07 Glossary Every term in the guide, in one plain sentence each. When a word trips you up mid-problem, this is where you look. Big-O A label for how fast the work grows as the input grows — the shape of the cost, not the exact time. amortized The average cost per operation over many operations, even","keywords":["glossary","dict","find"]},{"url":"reference/python-coding-round.html","page":"Python coding round","section":"Python coding round","tool":"reference","text":"Python coding round The problems and Python traps that show up most in mid-senior live coding. Plain answers first, then the keywords interviewers expect. Deep-link into pattern pages and a timed drill when you want the full treatment.","keywords":["python","coding","round"]},{"url":"reference/python-coding-round.html#how","page":"Python coding round","section":"What the round grades","tool":"reference","text":"01 What the round grades In ~40 minutes they watch four things: name the pattern fast, write correct Pythonic code, state time/space , and handle edge cases out loud. Trivia lists matter less than reaching for dict , set , collections , and heapq when they win. Warm-up arc say it “Clarify inputs and","keywords":["what","the","round","grades","dict","set","collections","heapq"]},{"url":"reference/python-coding-round.html#problems","page":"Python coding round","section":"Highest-frequency problems","tool":"reference","text":"02 Highest-frequency problems Frequency-weighted across FAANG-style reports (2025–2026). Learn these cold in Python. # Problem Pattern Answer (compress) Open 1 Two Sum Hash map One pass: store value→index; look up target − x . O(n)/O(n). Hashing · Drill 2 Buy/Sell Stock I One-pass greedy Track min_p","keywords":["highest","frequency","problems","target","min_price","price","min","next","start","cur","end","OrderedDict","dp","coins","for","amount","Counter","heapq","nlargest"]},{"url":"reference/python-coding-round.html#starters","page":"Python coding round","section":"Starter solutions (shapes to memorize)","tool":"reference","text":"03 Starter solutions (shapes to memorize) Two Sum two_sum.py Copy def two_sum(nums: list[int], target: int) -> list[int]: seen: dict[int, int] = {} for i, x in enumerate(nums): need = target - x if need in seen: return [seen[need], i] seen[x] = i raise ValueError(\"no pair\") # Time O(n) · Space O(n) ","keywords":["starter","solutions","shapes","to","memorize","def","two_sum","nums","list","int","target","seen","dict","for","in","enumerate","need","if","return","raise","ValueError","no","pair","Time","Space","OrderedDict","from","collections","import","class","LRUCache","__init__","self","capacity","cap","od","get","key","not","move_to_end"]},{"url":"reference/python-coding-round.html#python","page":"Python coding round","section":"Python traps that leak into coding","tool":"reference","text":"04 Python traps that leak into coding Mutable default Defaults bind once at def time. Use None and create inside the function. is vs == == is value ( __eq__ ). is is identity — reserve for None / True / False . Generators yield pauses; lazy streams beat huge lists. Generator expressions use () . Dec","keywords":["python","traps","that","leak","into","coding","def","None","is","__eq__","True","False","yield","functools","wraps","__name__","with","__enter__","__exit__","copy","deepcopy","multiprocessing","asyncio","Counter","defaultdict","deque","heapq","bisect","Bad","shared","list","across","calls","bad","acc","append","return","Good","good","if"]},{"url":"reference/python-coding-round.html#costs","page":"Python coding round","section":"Complexity you should say out loud","tool":"reference","text":"05 Complexity you should say out loud dict / set lookup·insert·delete → amortized O(1) list append → amortized O(1); insert(0) / pop(0) → O(n) — use deque heapq push/pop → O(log n); nlargest(k) → O(n log k) sort → O(n log n) Timsort (stable)","keywords":["complexity","you","should","say","out","loud","insert","deque","nlargest"]},{"url":"reference/python-coding-round.html#week","page":"Python coding round","section":"7-day drill (4y+)","tool":"reference","text":"06 7-day drill (4y+) Day Focus Do Pass bar 1 Hash + window Two Sum, longest unique, Buy/Sell Optimal + complexity 2 Intervals + heap Merge Intervals, Meeting Rooms II, Top K Sort rationale out loud 3 Graph Number of Islands (+ tree BFS if rusty) BFS vs DFS choice 4 Binary search Rotated search, one ","keywords":["day","drill"]},{"url":"reference/python-coding-round.html#say","page":"Python coding round","section":"Say it out loud","tool":"reference","text":"07 Say it out loud 60-second closer say it “I’d default to hash maps and sliding windows for array/string mediums, heaps for top-k, sort-plus-sweep for intervals, and BFS/DFS on grids. For design, LRU is dict plus doubly linked list — OrderedDict if they accept stdlib. I always state Big-O and avoid","keywords":["say","it","out","loud"]}];
